<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Triple Entente</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Triple Entente was the informal alignment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> formed in the years before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It developed from the Franco-Russian alliance and Britain's agreements with France in 1904 and Russia in 1907. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> treat German fears of encirclement by the Entente powers as a factor in the diplomacy and war that led to the empire's collapse.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870 to 1871, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a> to the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, and France built up its army to deter German aggression. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had been a member of the League of the Three Emperors of 1873 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, but rivalry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> prevented its renewal in 1887. Bismarck's secret <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinsurance-treaty">Reinsurance Treaty</a> with Russia lapsed in 1890, and Russian leaders, alarmed at their isolation, joined the Franco-Russian Alliance in 1894. It was designed as a counter to the Triple Alliance of Germany, Austria-Hungary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> kept to its policy of splendid isolation through the 1890s. By the early 1900s it saw the German threat as having grown sharply, and when overtures to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> were not returned it turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St. Petersburg</a>. In 1904 Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> signed five agreements on spheres of influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, mostly settling colonial disputes, which began the Entente cordiale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Tangier Crisis encouraged cooperation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from their shared fear of German expansion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>, which contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution-of-1905">Russian Revolution of 1905</a>. In 1907 the Anglo-Russian Entente tried to settle disputes over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> and to end the rivalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>, and it eased British fears about the Baghdad Railway. The Franco-Japanese Treaty of 1907 also formed part of the coalition. The Anglo-German naval race left an atmosphere of mutual hostility, and Lord Haldane's mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in February 1912 failed.</p><p>The Entente was not an alliance of mutual defence, unlike the Triple Alliance and the Franco-Russian Alliance, so <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was free in 1914 to make its own decisions. The Foreign Office official Eyre Crowe wrote that it was nothing more than a frame of mind. In 1914 all three powers entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allied Powers</a>, and on 4 September 1914 they declared that they would not make a separate peace.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Historians continue to debate how far the alliance system caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The alignment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> was controversial on both sides, since Russian conservatives distrusted the secular French and British and many French and British politicians disliked the tsarist regime. Mistrust lasted into the war, and British and French politicians expressed relief when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> abdicated in 1917. The British king withdrew an offer of asylum to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanovs</a>, and France never raised the subject. The Triple Entente was the coalition that fought the First World War against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Triple Entente” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Russian military modernisation was accelerating, the <b>Triple Entente</b> was hardening, and German leadership calculated that the window for a winnable war was closing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-empire">German Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Greece entered the First World War on the <b>Entente</b> side in 1917 and fought on the Macedonian front.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Abandoning the Triple Alliance with Germany and Austria-Hungary, Italy joined the <b>Entente</b> under the Treaty of London of April 1915 and entered the First World War that May.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>China had entered World War I on the side of the <b>Triple Entente</b> in 1917 and sent 140,000 labourers to the Western Front, yet the Treaty of Versailles awarded the rights to the German territories in Shandong, surrendered by Germany after the Siege of Tsingtao in 1914, to…</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>During the First World War, King Constantine I insisted on neutrality while Prime Minister Eleftherios Venizelos wanted to join the <b>Entente</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-national-defence">Provisional Government of National Defence</a></li></ul></section>
